1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,908
On 21 November, a spacecraft slipped into
orbit around Mercury.

2
00:00:05,278 --> 00:00:09,695
It arrived at a planet often thought of as
a dead, battered rock.

3
00:00:10,065 --> 00:00:11,069
But is it?

4
00:00:12,278 --> 00:00:14,583
The capture did not go perfectly.

5
00:00:14,953 --> 00:00:20,173
A software glitch put BepiColombo into
safe mode, and the braking burn ended

6
00:00:20,193 --> 00:00:20,587
early.

7
00:00:20,957 --> 00:00:28,596
Its science phase starts in April 2027, so
every clue in this video comes from older

8
00:00:28,616 --> 00:00:29,052
data,

9
00:00:29,071 --> 00:00:35,720
mostly from NASA's MESSENGER, which
orbited Mercury from 2011 to 2015.

10
00:00:36,441 --> 00:00:43,717
In 2026, new studies added to a decade of
hints that Mercury may not be fully dead.

11
00:00:44,087 --> 00:00:49,379
One of these clues is a set of bright
streaks nobody can fully explain yet.

12
00:00:49,749 --> 00:00:50,924
Let's start there.

13
00:00:51,643 --> 00:00:58,135
In early 2026, a team at the University of
Bern trained a machine learning tool on

14
00:00:58,155 --> 00:01:01,628
about one hundred thousand MESSENGER
images.

15
00:01:02,349 --> 00:01:07,351
It found about four hundred bright
streaks. They sit on sun facing slopes of

16
00:01:07,371 --> 00:01:08,277
young craters,

17
00:01:09,349 --> 00:01:14,376
and many begin at hollows, the shallow
pits that dot Mercury's surface.

18
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:18,319
The proposed explanation is volatile loss.

19
00:01:18,689 --> 00:01:23,519
Gas, perhaps sulfur or other light
elements, escaping from the rock and

20
00:01:23,539 --> 00:01:25,090
leaving streaks behind.

21
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:30,577
Now the caution. About ninety three
percent of the streaks are in the north,

22
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:36,292
and the team ties that to how well
MESSENGER imaged each region.

23
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:42,187
About ten percent are not in craters at
all, so other mechanisms may be at work.

24
00:01:43,810 --> 00:01:48,142
An outside scientist called the link to
volatiles plausible.

25
00:01:48,512 --> 00:01:49,367
Plausible.

26
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:50,826
Not proven.

27
00:01:51,196 --> 00:01:53,757
And nobody has watched a streak form.

28
00:01:54,477 --> 00:01:58,254
Those hollows are a clue in their own
right, and they are not new.

29
00:01:58,624 --> 00:02:02,103
MESSENGER found them back in 2011.

30
00:02:02,477 --> 00:02:05,998
What is newer is the question of how old
they are.

31
00:02:07,477 --> 00:02:11,980
NASA says there are almost no small
craters inside the hollows.

32
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:15,679
That means they are much younger than the
ground around them.

33
00:02:16,477 --> 00:02:22,388
The dating is uncertain, but NASA asks a
bold question: may they still be forming?

34
00:02:23,477 --> 00:02:26,508
A 2025 study agrees.

35
00:02:26,878 --> 00:02:31,487
It found most hollows are in an early
stage, and likely still evolving.

36
00:02:31,857 --> 00:02:35,336
The authors say BepiColombo can look for
change.

37
00:02:35,706 --> 00:02:37,499
Keep the hollows in mind.

38
00:02:37,869 --> 00:02:39,534
We will come back to them.

39
00:02:40,255 --> 00:02:41,792
Now the second study.

40
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:43,934
Mercury is shrinking.

41
00:02:44,304 --> 00:02:45,948
That part is not new.

42
00:02:46,318 --> 00:02:51,439
As the planet cooled, its crust buckled
into long cliffs called thrust faults.

43
00:02:51,809 --> 00:02:53,795
What is new is how much.

44
00:02:54,318 --> 00:03:00,625
In September 2026, researchers at the
German Aerospace Center argued that rough,

45
00:03:00,645 --> 00:03:05,370
rubble covered terrain hides many of these
faults from view.

46
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:11,723
Earlier estimates said Mercury lost up to
about sixteen kilometres of width.

47
00:03:12,093 --> 00:03:14,761
The new study says up to twenty three.

48
00:03:15,131 --> 00:03:20,695
That is more than two Everests, not
removed, but squeezed, as the crust folded

49
00:03:20,715 --> 00:03:21,554
over itself.

50
00:03:22,274 --> 00:03:24,537
Let me be plain about who says what.

51
00:03:24,907 --> 00:03:29,111
The streaks hint at recent, and possibly
ongoing, gas loss.

52
00:03:30,274 --> 00:03:35,993
This shrinking study is about the past. It
tells us how much Mercury has shrunk over

53
00:03:36,013 --> 00:03:37,223
its whole history,

54
00:03:38,274 --> 00:03:43,953
not how fast it shrinks now. The question
of today belongs to an older study.

55
00:03:44,323 --> 00:03:50,138
In 2023, researchers at the Open
University mapped hundreds of small

56
00:03:50,158 --> 00:03:52,026
trenches in the crust.

57
00:03:52,746 --> 00:03:57,584
They estimated these features to be three
hundred million years old or younger,

58
00:03:58,746 --> 00:04:02,999
and read them as a sign of contraction
that is still going on.

59
00:04:03,746 --> 00:04:09,230
But the team also said that this reading
may be refuted by BepiColombo imagery.

60
00:04:09,746 --> 00:04:14,121
That is an honest scientist, leaving the
door open to being wrong.

61
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,231
The third area is volcanism.

62
00:04:17,601 --> 00:04:21,506
Ancient volcanism on Mercury is widely
accepted.

63
00:04:21,876 --> 00:04:25,675
The open question is whether anything is
active today.

64
00:04:26,395 --> 00:04:32,716
In September 2026, conference abstracts
suggested hints of buried volcanic ash,

65
00:04:32,736 --> 00:04:37,532
and of explosive eruptions at bright
patches on the surface.

66
00:04:37,902 --> 00:04:44,177
They also described a tentative, very
large cone of volcanic debris inside the

67
00:04:44,197 --> 00:04:45,306
Caloris basin.

68
00:04:46,026 --> 00:04:47,222
Be careful here.

69
00:04:47,592 --> 00:04:53,285
These deposits describe explosive
volcanism in the past, not activity today,

70
00:04:53,305 --> 00:04:55,465
and their ages are uncertain.

71
00:04:55,836 --> 00:04:59,784
These are also conference abstracts, not
peer reviewed papers.

72
00:05:00,154 --> 00:05:02,822
Nobody has seen an eruption happening.

73
00:05:03,542 --> 00:05:06,530
So where does that leave the label, dead
planet?

74
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:08,245
Let's be exact.

75
00:05:08,615 --> 00:05:09,662
Landforms.

76
00:05:10,032 --> 00:05:10,865
Chemistry.

77
00:05:11,235 --> 00:05:13,028
Counts of small features.

78
00:05:13,398 --> 00:05:15,362
All of it is indirect.

79
00:05:15,732 --> 00:05:20,064
Nobody has seen an eruption, a new cliff,
or a new streak form.

80
00:05:20,784 --> 00:05:24,028
So the evidence does not prove Mercury is
alive.

81
00:05:24,398 --> 00:05:27,386
What it does is put pressure on the word
dead.

82
00:05:28,784 --> 00:05:30,876
The better word may be quiet.

83
00:05:31,246 --> 00:05:33,743
And quiet is not the same as finished.

84
00:05:34,784 --> 00:05:36,385
So how do we settle it?

85
00:05:36,755 --> 00:05:42,930
BepiColombo is a European and Japanese
mission, and only the second ever to orbit

86
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:43,541
Mercury.

87
00:05:43,911 --> 00:05:48,798
It launched in 2018, so this arrival took
about eight years.

88
00:05:49,518 --> 00:05:56,559
ESA says the science phase should start in
April 2027, though dates may change.

89
00:05:57,279 --> 00:06:02,858
Now think about what its tools can add.
The European orbiter carries a laser

90
00:06:02,878 --> 00:06:03,595
altimeter,

91
00:06:03,615 --> 00:06:08,636
and study author Nishiyama told the
Associated Press that it should confirm

92
00:06:08,656 --> 00:06:09,577
the shrinking.

93
00:06:10,297 --> 00:06:15,612
It also carries a high-resolution camera
that can compare today's surface with

94
00:06:15,632 --> 00:06:16,912
MESSENGER's images.

95
00:06:17,632 --> 00:06:19,468
And here is the callback.

96
00:06:19,838 --> 00:06:23,189
Remember the hollows, young and maybe
still forming?

97
00:06:23,558 --> 00:06:27,015
That is the kind of camera that could
catch one changing.

98
00:06:28,558 --> 00:06:36,723
The key comparison is MESSENGER, from 2011
to 2015, against BepiColombo, more than a

99
00:06:36,743 --> 00:06:37,989
decade later.

100
00:06:38,359 --> 00:06:40,046
A second, sharper look.

101
00:06:40,416 --> 00:06:43,660
Comparing the two is how we can search for
change.

102
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:48,819
My guess is that if anything changes, it
will be small and slow.

103
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:51,280
A hollow edge that moved.

104
00:06:51,650 --> 00:06:53,870
A streak that was not there before.

105
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:58,051
But it is entirely possible we will see no
change at all.

106
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:04,210
If nothing changes at the camera's
resolution, that would at least put limits

107
00:07:04,230 --> 00:07:06,037
on how active Mercury can be.

108
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:11,755
Think about what it would mean if the edge
of a single hollow had moved.

109
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:18,537
Not a theory about the past, but a change
we could point to, between two sets of

110
00:07:18,557 --> 00:07:19,120
pictures.

111
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:23,859
That one small shift would turn a clue
into a measurement.

112
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:27,045
The next step is close.

113
00:07:27,415 --> 00:07:31,705
On 9 to 10 December, the two orbiters are
due to separate.

114
00:07:32,424 --> 00:07:35,945
The honest answer today is that we do not
know yet.

115
00:07:37,424 --> 00:07:42,973
If you want to follow the story as the
first science images arrive, subscribe to

116
00:07:42,993 --> 00:07:44,295
Rebel Minds Cosmos.

117
00:07:45,424 --> 00:07:48,711
Dead, quiet, or something we have not
named yet?

118
00:07:49,081 --> 00:07:50,490
Tell me in the comments.
